--------------------------------------------------------------------------
README file for OOSSIM
Created Nov 27, 2008 by Thomas Johnson
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Designed by Steve Bickerton, this program simulates the detection of
Kuiper Belt Objects (KBOs) by serendipitous occultation of background
stars. See Bickerton (2007) for a full run down of the methods employed
here.

The main user interface of the program is located in the evalTarget dir.
These scripts tie all of the components together and perform the target
evaluation, printing the results to standard output both for KBOs and
Oort Cloud Objects (OCOs). Check out the README file in that dir for a
more complete description of the different scripts and which ones are
important to actually running the simulations. However, if one is wishing
to run the simulation on the computing cluster at HIA, the script
jobSubmit.pl was designed to automate this process, allowing for rapid
creation and submission of jobs. Furthermore, a certain parameter can be
varied over a range specified in some ASCII data file containing the
specific values, separated by a carriage return (hitting 'enter'). Type
./jobSubmit.pl at the console for the help message, or see below for more
information.

INSTALLATION-------------------------------------------------------------

To install OOSSIM a number of steps must be taken, all of which may be 
summarized in a few broad steps: setting up the environment, installing 
the pre-requisite packages and libraries and compiling and installing the 
libraries and executables.

1. SETTING UP THE ENVIRONMENT

Create the following directory structure in your home directory:

~/usr
~/usr/bin
~/usr/include
~/usr/lib
~/usr/libperl
~/usr/libperl/Local ***note the capital on Local

Next set the following environment variables in the configuration file of
whatever shell program you use. E.g. if you use csh, the file will be
'~/.cshrc', or if you use bash the file will be '~/.bashrc'.

FLUXCALC=/path/to/oossim/root/directory/fluxCalc
PERL5LIB=${HOME}/usr/libperl

Note that the manner in which environment variables are defined will depend
on the type of shell you are using, so a quick google search to determine
this would be smart.

2. INSTALLING THE PREREQUISITES

The following C libraries are needed by OOSSIM to function properly:

Fastest Fourier Transform in the West - http://www.FFTW.org
The GNU GSL Library for C and C++ - http://www.gnu.org/software/gsl/
The FITSIO Library - http://heasarc.nasa.gov/lheasoft/fitsio/fitsio.html

For the above libraries, the header files should be in ~/usr/include, the
library files should go in ~/usr/lib and any executables should go in
~/usr/bin. Ensure this is the case in the corresponding make files for each
library before you install!

One package of note that is also needed is scipy, a package of scientific
tools for python. As of the most recent python versions it is included, so
ensure your python installation is up to date.

3. INSTALLING THE OOSSIM COMPONENTS

The following section will assume the main OOSSIM installation is located
at '~/OOSSIM/'

~/OOSSIM/analysis and ~/OOSSIM/utility both contain numerous perl modules
that need to be installed to the libperl/Local directory. This can be done
by running 'make install' within each directory.

~/OOSSIM/cdiffracsim and ~/OOSSIM/Canalysis are both written in C and must
be compiled accordingly. 'make' will compile the programs, 'make test' will
ensure the compilation was performed correctly and 'make install' will 
copy the executables to ~/usr/bin

~/OOSSIM/fluxCalc is written in Fortran, and must be compiled accordingly
as well. Assuming one has g77 or f77 installed as fortran compilers, typing
'make' in the directory is sufficient.

Once the above steps have been completed, the scripts may be run from the
~/OOSSIM/evalTarget directory. It is reccomended that the output be piped
into a log file instead of the terminal as the amount of text printed is
rather large. See ~/OOSSIM/evalTarget/README for more details.